{"id":"effect-4d873537-31d3-5204-b2df-3a933c4ad02e","sessionId":"613e7f89-916e-f42e-451f-8370bdf6574b","turnId":"turn-203fd02bea1d2028380117cd4c2eb7ff","inputEntryId":"user-turn-203fd02bea1d2028380117cd4c2eb7ff-1","createdAt":1794274004157,"updatedAt":1794274004170,"generationStatus":"completed","relatedEntryId":null,"type":"effect","title":"file_system.read_file","detail":{"toolName":"file_system.read_file","display":{"summary":"Reading README.md","content":null,"suffix":"","verb":"Reading","message":"README.md","settledVerb":"Read","settledMessage":"README.md","statusText":"Reading file"},"kind":"file_read","input":{"filePath":"README.md","offset":null,"limit":2000}},"state":{"status":"completed","outputText":"","output":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/h-X6Ho0j/README.md","content":"# stories-api\n\nA small story library behind `POST /stories` (Lambda + DynamoDB table `stories`,\npartition key `slug`). `src/app.py` is the whole service: `handler()` parses\nthe request and `publish()` stores the story together with its three most\nsimilar existing stories (`similar: [{slug, score}]`), so the reader page can\nshow \"related\" without any query at read time. `logs/error.log` is the\nproduction log from the first two publishes after the deploy.\n\nTests use an in-memory table (`tests/fake_dynamo.py`) that validates items the\nway boto3 does, so no AWS account is needed. Run them with\n`python3 -m unittest discover tests` from the repo root.\n","numLines":12,"startLine":1,"requestedOffset":null,"requestedLimit":2000,"totalLines":null,"wasTruncated":false},"durationMs":0.0,"approvalSource":"bypass","approvalType":"always","display":{"success":true,"verb":"Read","message":"12 lines from README.md","warnings":[],"approvalNote":null,"suffix":""},"decision":"execute"}}
{"id":"assistant-15ebf86b-1da1-54cd-bada-1dd52d2906ad","sessionId":"613e7f89-916e-f42e-451f-8370bdf6574b","turnId":"turn-203fd02bea1d2028380117cd4c2eb7ff","inputEntryId":"user-turn-203fd02bea1d2028380117cd4c2eb7ff-1","createdAt":1794274009141,"updatedAt":1794274009141,"generationStatus":"completed","relatedEntryId":null,"type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I understand the issue. Let me analyze:\n\n1. The first publish works fine (slug: `why-my-cron-ran-twice`)\n2. The second publish fails with `TypeError: Float types are not supported. Use Decimal types instead.`\n\nLooking at the code:\n- In `app.py`, line 56: `\"published_at\": int(time.time())` - this creates an integer timestamp\n- In `app.py`, line 57: `\"similar\": related(story, existing)` - this returns a list of dicts with `slug` and `score`\n- In `app.py`, line 26: `return round(0.7 * tag_score + 0.3 * word_score, 4)` - this returns a **float** for the score\n\nThe problem is that `similarity()` returns a float (from `round()`), and when this float is stored in DynamoDB via boto3, it fails because DynamoDB doesn't support float types - it requires Decimal.\n\nThe first publish works because `existing` is empty, so `related()` returns an empty list `[]`, which has no float scores. The second publish fails because now there's one existing story, so `related()` computes a similarity score (a float) and tries to store it.\n\nLet me verify this by running the test:"}],"source":"harness","userDisplayContent":null,"outcome":{"type":"committed"}}
